<p>U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio was due to meet Pope Leo XIV at the Vatican on Thursday, May 7, in the first meeting in nearly a year between the pope and a Trump cabinet official. The closed-door meeting at the Apostolic Palace was expected to last about half an hour, followed by talks between Rubio and Vatican Secretary of State Cardinal Pietro Parolin. The visit followed weeks of public criticism by President Trump after Pope Leo criticized the U.S.-Israeli war on Iran and the Trump administration’s immigration policies. Trump said on Monday that the pope was “endangering a lot of Catholics” and falsely suggested Leo supported Iran obtaining nuclear weapons; the pope rejected the claim and said the Catholic Church has long opposed nuclear weapons. Rubio, who is Catholic and also serves as national security adviser, said before the trip that the Vatican meeting had been scheduled before the latest dispute and that he expected to discuss religious freedom, Cuba and other diplomatic issues. Parolin said the talks would include recent events and international questions, adding that “we cannot avoid touching these topics”.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DTDNhLOsg-7iO5jm9lR_be6PSsq2yz78DypPf7HLCuIZ3aDYXyy5RlqyX6GRgtoNy0W_rTeuZpMFYxLxJbJJHnV5HQk3QFXEjoiCgpzFP_AFMu7dPJGS-djjiLaTFTYgWUeDu8i0ZBgA_2tntVS_dm2A48wEsXkq6hg8kDsmuLD3WEWVbpSh1uAAX1Dtxqehuh1u-y7_HJcmLq9ua2kxBwXFiAWO8Ph6CcQK4gxe-HXdQw_839_1qGVsChbcXD9IySUJQbjCIPp9jY8DsQ' alt='U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio greets Pope Leo XIV at the Vatican in a May 19, 2025, file photo.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Vatican reaction: Parolin described Trump’s attacks on Leo as “a bit strange, to say the least,” while saying he did not want to make personal judgments.</li><li>Papal anniversary: Leo, the first U.S.-born pope, marks his first year leading the 1.4-billion-member Catholic Church on Friday.</li><li>Policy agenda: Parolin said discussions would also cover Latin America and Lebanon, areas where 